**Read:** [Introdução à Mecânica dos Fluidos e aos Fenômenos de Transporte — Gobbi and colleagues](../sources/Mecanica-dos-Fluidos-e-Transportes-Gobbi-Dias.lingua#r_1F9KHVCP86X68RR7DTZASE5F1J), chapter 1, especially physical quantities and units; [University Physics Volume 1 — OpenStax](../sources/University-Physics-Volume-1.lingua#r_2W7QCEHZ45XDB3AWVG9NTV36F6), density, pressure, flow rate and viscosity sections.

Connect the vocabulary to a measured quantity. Massa específica is mass density ρ in kg/m³; vazão volumétrica is volume flow rate Q in m³/s; vazão mássica is mass flow rate in kg/s. Pressão is pressure in Pa; tensão de cisalhamento is shear stress, also in Pa. Viscosidade dinâmica μ has units Pa·s and viscosidade cinemática ν=μ/ρ has units m²/s.

A quantity with the same unit can have a different physical meaning. Pressure and shear stress act differently on a surface. Energy density and pressure share units, but a pressure reading does not measure every form of energy in a volume. Distinguish discharge from speed: Q is a surface integral of normal velocity, not a velocity at one point.

Write the units and reference state beside every input. Convert a volume flow only after specifying whether it describes the actual fluid conditions or a stated reference condition. Record whether a quoted friction factor is Darcy or Fanning before using a pressure-loss equation.

